// time module: wall clock, monotonic clock, sleep.
//
// C implementation: lib/time.c (lazily dlopen'd on first call).
//
//   now_ms()       wall-clock (CLOCK_REALTIME) milliseconds
//   monotonic_ms() CLOCK_MONOTONIC milliseconds, same source as vm.time_ms
//   sleep(ms)      suspend the calling actor for ~ms milliseconds,
//                  returns 0; non-int arg -> -1 (the C side guards the
//                  arg: the param below is deliberately untyped — a
//                  lowercase `float`-style annotation currently
//                  quantifies as a type variable at the type layer,
//                  language changes are frozen this phase)
//
// sleep SUSPENSION MECHANISM (v2, poll-based): the calling actor yields
// and arms a deadline on the scheduler's poll loop (the SAME mechanism
// that wakes net_connect timeouts); when the deadline passes the actor
// is re-enqueued and sleep returns 0. No worker thread is ever blocked,
// so any number of actors can sleep concurrently on one worker.
//
// Why not recv_after(ms) (v1 rationale, still valid): recv_after is
// mailbox-bound — a message arriving mid-wait wakes the caller early AND
// consumes the message. sleep must leave the mailbox untouched (messages
// sent during the sleep queue up and are received afterwards) and wait
// the full duration. The poll-deadline mechanism gives exactly that.

external fn time.now_ms() -> int
external fn time.monotonic_ms() -> int
external fn time.sleep(ms) -> int
